 "notes": "37 stations (lines 736-773). There are 25 stars, which matches the text. The only † is on Longstone, the only station with no star and no keeper that also carries a mark; the footnote sends readers to earlier Reports for its details. There are no map numbers, no light characters and no sub-headings inside the list, so coast is null throughout. The whole list sits under the section heading EAST COAST OF ENGLAND.\n\nChoices I made:\n- Fragments are copied with the left-margin indentation removed. The spacing inside each line, the braces and the final full stops are kept.\n- In the keeper names I dropped the full stop that ends each line. It is list punctuation, not part of the name.\n- Gull and Southsand Head each print \"X and / Y\" inside a brace. I split each into two keepers and left out the joining \"and\".\n- For Casquets and Hanois I put the bracketed island, \"(Alderney)\" and \"(Guernsey)\", in place without the brackets.\n- I kept \"(Newsand)\" in the name \"Spurn (Newsand)\" because it names the sandbank the vessel lies on, not a county or island.\n- I kept \"Teesmouth\" in the name \"No. 5 Buoy, Teesmouth\". \"No. 5\" is part of the buoy's name, not a committee map number.\n- \"Whitby, High,\" is kept with its commas.\n\nLayout oddity: line 757 has one more leading space before \"*Corton\" than the other starred rows. It looks like a typesetting or transcription slip, and the star is clearly meant for Corton.\n\nSpellings to check, all copied as printed:\n- \"Hasbrough\" (Happisburgh) appears twice, once as L.V. (starred, line 751) and once as L.H. (line 752). These are two separate stations.\n- \"Orfordness\" is printed as one word.\n- These names may be misprints and are worth checking against other years: \"Samuel Sheet\", \"Thomas Randule\" (Randall?), \"J. G. Fornman\" (Forman?), \"Charles Perfrement\" and \"W. T. Cotton\" at Corton (the likeness of the two words may be a coincidence).\n- George Rees (Lynn Well) and Thomas Rees (Eastside) share a surname.",
